"""Render a standalone, auditable report from completed-run summaries."""
from pathlib import Path
import base64, html, json, time
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
REPO='https://github.com/NousVolition/My-Sources-Project-ALL'
TAG='navier-stokes-stress-2026-10-09'
def fmt(x):return '—' if x is None else f'{x:.4g}'
def image(name,caption):
    b=base64.b64encode((ROOT/name).read_bytes()).decode()
    return f'<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64,{b}" alt="{html.escape(caption)}"><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>'
def table(headers,rows):return '<div class="table"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join(f'<th>{h}</th>' for h in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{c}</td>' for c in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'

def main():
    a=json.loads((ROOT/'summary.json').read_text());v=json.loads((ROOT/'verification.json').read_text());test=json.loads((ROOT/'validation.json').read_text())
    rows=a['runs'];fine=[]
    for nu in [.02,.005,.001]:
        candidates=[r for r in rows if r['settings']['family']=='kida' and r['settings']['nu']==nu and r['settings']['epsilon']==0 and r['settings']['dt']==.01]
        if candidates:fine.append(max(candidates,key=lambda r:r['settings']['n']))
    grid=[c for c in a['comparisons'] if c['kind']=='grid' and any(c['coarse'].startswith('kida-n'+str(n)+'-') for n in [96,128,192]) and '-e0-' in c['coarse']]
    tim=[c for c in a['comparisons'] if c['kind']=='time'];failed_local=sum(r['last_local_prefix']<r['last_t'] for r in rows)
    maxbudget=max(r['max_energy_residual'] for r in rows);maxdiv=max(r['max_divergence'] for r in rows)
    body=f'''<header><div class="eyebrow">NOUS VOLITION · COMPUTATIONAL FLUID DYNAMICS</div>
    <h1>Push the flow.<br>Test the apparent failure.</h1>
    <p class="lede">A stress test of the actual three-dimensional incompressible Navier–Stokes equations, with molecular experiments kept separate.</p>
    <p>{v['completed_runs']} / {v['planned_runs']} planned runs completed · up to {max(r['settings']['n'] for r in rows)}³ grid points · {v['verified_fields']} saved fields verified</p></header>
    <section class="callout"><h2>What this establishes</h2><p>No breakdown of the Navier–Stokes equations is established. Strong vortex growth can be measured, but later low-viscosity results encounter spatial-resolution limits. {failed_local} completed runs lose at least one local accuracy screen before their endpoint. Finer-grid agreement and the independent time-step checks determine which earlier observations are usable.</p><p><strong>A failed numerical screen is an experimental result about the computation. It is not evidence that the physical equations have failed.</strong></p></section>
    <h2>What was actually pushed</h2><p>The experiment starts from two smooth, periodic vortex fields: Kida–Pelz and three-dimensional Taylor–Green. Lowering the positive viscosity allows stretching to create finer structures. Grids from 32³ to 256³ test spatial sensitivity. The original 33-case matrix was followed by six explicitly recorded refinements after the GPU became available. Small, energy-matched flow perturbations test amplification without injecting extra initial kinetic energy. No artificial molecule, heavy particle, body force, negative viscosity, hyperviscosity, amplitude clipping or modified constitutive law is introduced.</p>
    <p>All quantities use dimensionless model units. The calculation does not specify a water temperature, physical length, Mach number or molecular mean free path. It tests the continuum equations; it is not a calibrated water experiment.</p>'''
    body+=image('stress.png','Vorticity growth and the fraction of enstrophy near the spectral cutoff. The dashed red line is the predeclared 1% screening threshold. Curves beyond a failed screen are retained to expose numerical sensitivity.')
    if (ROOT/'vorticity-slices.png').exists():body+=image('vorticity-slices.png','Endpoint slices at low viscosity show how changing spatial resolution changes the recorded small-scale structure. Passing a local screen alone is insufficient: consult the cross-grid comparisons.')
    body+='<h2>Finest Kida–Pelz runs</h2>'+table(['Viscosity','Grid','Peak vorticity','Peak / initial','Energy remaining','Maximum cutoff fraction','Last local pass before first failure'],[[fmt(r['settings']['nu']),str(r['settings']['n'])+'³',fmt(r['peak_W']),fmt(r['W_amplification']),f"{100*r['energy_remaining_fraction']:.2f}%",f"{100*r['max_tail_Z']:.3f}%",fmt(r['last_local_prefix'])] for r in fine])
    body+='<p>The listed peaks include the full diagnostic run, even when late values are unresolved. They must not be interpreted as converged continuum peaks.</p>'
    body+='<h2>Where cross-grid agreement holds</h2>'+table(['Comparison','Last contiguous checked time passing all screens','Final velocity-field difference','Final peak-vorticity difference'],[[c['coarse'].split('-e')[0]+' → '+c['fine'].split('-')[1],fmt(c['last_contiguous_pass_time']),f"{100*c['points'][-1]['velocity_relative_L2']:.3f}%",f"{100*c['points'][-1]['W_relative_difference']:.3f}%"] for c in grid])
    body+='<p>Full-field comparisons occur every 0.5 model-time units for Kida–Pelz and every 1 unit for Taylor–Green. Local diagnostics occur every 0.1. The reported prefix stops at the first failure; a later pass does not erase it. Passing at these saved times is evidence of numerical consistency, not a rigorous error bound between observations.</p>'
    body+=image('convergence.png','Spatial comparisons include both common Fourier modes and fine-grid modes absent on the coarse grid. Time-step comparisons rerun the same starting field with a smaller maximum step.')
    body+='<h3>Smaller-time-step controls</h3>'+table(['Run','Smaller maximum step','Largest field difference','Largest peak-vorticity difference','All temporal comparisons pass'],[[c['coarse'].replace('kida-',''),c['fine'].split('-dt')[-1],f"{100*max(p['velocity_relative_L2'] for p in c['points']):.6f}%",f"{100*max(p['W_relative_difference'] for p in c['points']):.6f}%",str(all(p['comparison_pass'] for p in c['points']))] for c in tim])
    body+='<p>These temporal comparisons isolate time integration on the same grid. A temporal pass does not override spatial or local-screen failures.</p>'
    body+='<h2>What the energy budget can—and cannot—tell us</h2>'+f'<p>The largest recorded relative energy-budget residual is {maxbudget:.3g}; the largest divergence RMS is {maxdiv:.3g}. These check faithful evolution of the discrete incompressible system. They do not establish that its spatial cutoff resolves the continuum flow. A truncated system can conserve its budget accurately while missing important small scales.</p>'
    body+='<p>Energy and enstrophy are spatial averages: E = ½⟨|u|²⟩ and Z = ½⟨|ω|²⟩. The unforced budget is dE/dt = −2νZ. Enstrophy production is ⟨ωᵢωⱼ∂ⱼuᵢ⟩, opposed by ν⟨|∇ω|²⟩. No rescaling of the velocity or energy is applied during evolution.</p>'
    body+=image('budgets.png','At 96³, compare energy decay, vortex-stretching production minus viscous enstrophy loss, and the independently accumulated energy budget.')
    body+='<h2>Small changes, different futures</h2><p>Four fixed random directions perturb the Kida–Pelz velocity field on 48³. A selected direction is repeated at amplitudes 10⁻⁶, 10⁻³ and 10⁻² on 64³ and 96³, with the 10⁻³ case also checked at 128³. Each perturbation is divergence free, has zero mean, is initially orthogonal to the baseline velocity in the energy inner product, and is normalized to preserve initial total energy. Consequently these are different initial velocity fields, not different masses.</p><p>Amplification is ||uε(t)−u₀(t)||₂ / ||uε(0)−u₀(0)||₂. Participation is ⟨|δu|²⟩²/⟨|δu|⁴⟩, the effective volume fraction occupied by the difference. Because the starting perturbations are broad Fourier modes, participation does not measure transmission from a localized molecule. There are no particle labels and no molecular leadership claim.</p>'
    body+=image('perturbations.png','Finite-window sensitivity and participation. The four seeds sample directions around one initial flow; they are not four independent physical systems. Check summary.json for numerical validity and the descriptive four-seed bootstrap interval.')
    body+='<h3>Does the small response itself converge?</h3><p>A small response needs a stricter check than agreement of the much larger total velocity. We therefore compare δu itself across grids, normalized by the fine-grid δu norm, and compare the amplification factors. This extra analysis control is recorded in analysis-amendment.json.</p>'+table(['Response comparison','Last contiguous checked time passing response and local screens','Final response-field difference'],[[c['coarse'].replace('kida-','').replace('-s1-dt0.01','')+' → '+c['fine'].split('-')[1],fmt(c['last_contiguous_pass_time']),f"{100*c['points'][-1]['response_field_relative_L2']:.3f}%"] for c in a.get('response_comparisons',[])])
    body+='<h2>A second family of initial conditions</h2><p>Taylor–Green runs test whether the diagnosis depends on the Kida–Pelz geometry. The integration stops at model time 4, earlier than the full late-time turbulence benchmark often used in the literature. This is not a complete high-Reynolds-number benchmark validation.</p>'
    body+=image('taylor-green.png','Taylor–Green vorticity and spectral cutoff diagnostics on three grids and two positive viscosities.')
    body+='<h2>Equations and reproducibility</h2><p>∂u/∂t = P(u × ω) + ν∇²u, ω = ∇×u, ∇·u = 0, on [0,2π)³. P is the divergence-free Fourier pressure projection. Density is constant. The rotational nonlinearity is evaluated pseudospectrally with strict componentwise |k| &lt; N/3 truncation. Time stepping uses classical fourth-order Runge–Kutta, an advective step limit and a diffusive stability limit. Both nonlinear transfer and ordinary viscosity remain active.</p><p>Kida–Pelz starts with ux = sin(x)[cos(3y)cos(z) − cos(y)cos(3z)], with uy and uz given by cyclic permutation. Taylor–Green starts with (sin(x)cos(y)cos(z), −cos(x)sin(y)cos(z), 0). These finite-mode fields are identical across grids; they avoid the periodic-join discontinuity diagnosed in the older project start.</p>'
    body+=f'<p>All {test["tests"]} implementation tests passed: incompressibility and instantaneous energy balance; initial energy and cross-grid identity; exact viscous decay of ABC and two-dimensional Taylor–Green fields; fourth-order convergence for an advected, decaying ABC field; rejection of nonpositive viscosity. All {v["verified_fields"]} raw checkpoint hashes and recomputed energies/enstrophies passed: {v["passed"]}.</p>'
    av=json.loads((ROOT/'analysis-validation.json').read_text())
    body+=f'<p>The {av["tests"]} analysis tests passed: {av["passed"]}. Analytic comparisons verify that shared Fourier modes and unresolved fine-grid modes are counted once, and that later screen passes cannot erase a first failure.</p>'
    body+='<p>Screen thresholds were written before evolution: cutoff enstrophy below 1%; relative energy residual below 0.1%; divergence RMS below 10⁻⁹; CFL at most 0.5; grid differences below 1% in the velocity field and 5% in maximum vorticity. Time-step comparison thresholds are 0.2% and 1%. These are deliberately explicit descriptive tolerances, not proven bounds.</p>'
    body+='<h2>What would justify a stronger claim</h2><p>A candidate physical concentration would have to persist with increasing spatial resolution, smaller time steps, a sufficiently resolved spectral tail, consistent local geometry and the correct energy budget. An independent numerical formulation would strengthen the evidence. Finite computations cannot establish a finite-time singularity, and a finite Fourier system cannot itself exhibit the continuum singularity at issue. We do not fit a blowup time to unresolved peaks.</p><p>This is a new fluid experiment. The earlier nine-water isotope study remains separate. No proposed extreme-mass molecular experiment was run after the user redirected the work to Navier–Stokes. No statement about molecular evolution or a single isotope’s effect follows from these fluid runs.</p>'
    if (ROOT/'gpu-validation.json').exists():
        gv=json.loads((ROOT/'gpu-validation.json').read_text());bc=json.loads((ROOT/'backend-comparison.json').read_text())
        body+=f'<h2>Execution backend control</h2><p>The complete primary matrix uses {html.escape(gv["gpu"])} with CuPy {gv["cupy"]}, keeping double precision and the original algorithm. Six CPU/GPU RHS and trajectory checks passed: {gv["passed"]}. There are {len(bc["comparisons"])} overlapping complete production runs, with {bc["cpu_fields_verified"]} CPU checkpoint hashes checked; their whole-field comparisons passed: {bc["passed"]}. The optional acceleration was added during execution because CPU timings were expensive. gpu-amendment.json records this execution change. The large-grid GPU diagnostics were also checked against the CPU definitions in refinement-diagnostics-validation.json. The published experiment’s execution-events.json records an interrupted final control restarted from its original initial conditions. Completed and interrupted CPU runs remain separate; they are not added to the count of 33 original cases plus six follow-up cases.</p>'
    body+='<h2>The network images supplied during execution</h2><p>The supplied Figure 2 and phase-oscillator equation match <a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/network-physiology/articles/10.3389/fnetp.2023.1276401/full">Meyer-Ortmanns (2023), Heteroclinic networks for brain dynamics</a>. In that figure, nodes denote saddle states in the state space; they are not nine molecules. In the phase equation, wᵢⱼ is a coupling weight from oscillator j to i, not a mass. A network of system states must be distinguished from a network of interacting components.</p><p>For our unforced, periodic, zero-mean Navier–Stokes setup, dE/dt = −ν⟨|∇u|²⟩. Poincaré on this 2π box gives dE/dt ≤ −2νE. Thus E(t) ≤ E(0)exp(−2νt) for smooth solutions, with the corresponding energy inequality for admissible weak solutions. A nonzero recurrent orbit or a closed cycle of nonzero steady states is excluded by strict energy decay in this setup. The same energy argument applies to the exact finite Fourier system. Nonzero constant means would require separating the conserved mean, which is zero in these runs.</p><p>Transient visits to similar vortex shapes remain possible, and normalized shapes can recur while amplitude decays. Neither establishes a heteroclinic attractor in the full velocity state. A future state-transition analysis would need resolved, densely saved trajectories, out-of-sample state definitions, dwell-time and transition uncertainty, symmetry-aware distances, and direct invariant-state/stability checks. </p><p>A follow-up prompted by the supplied memory passage would compare held-out predictions of the next coarse state conditioned on the current state alone against predictions also conditioned on earlier states, matching dwell time and time since initialization. It would require independent trajectories, adequate transition counts, uncertainty across trajectories, and checks that apparent memory is not caused by unresolved dynamics or discarded state information. Full deterministic velocity evolution and its coarse state labels are different objects. No stochastic noise was added to the present fluid runs. </p><p>A second supplied passage suggests learning a state network from data. Such an inferred network would need evaluation on held-out initial conditions and comparison with simpler transition models; matching its training sequence would not verify the assumed heteroclinic mechanism or establish physical learning in the fluid. These analyses were not run here. Introducing sustained forcing could support different dynamics within forced Navier–Stokes, but would be a new, separately specified experiment. No oscillator equations or designed nine-state network were substituted for the fluid PDE. The supplied formal definition requires invariant saddles and trajectories in the intersection of an outgoing unstable manifold and an incoming stable manifold. A graph of observed transitions alone does not verify those objects. Distinct-state connections require nonconstant connecting trajectories; the equilibrium self-intersection is not evidence of switching. </p><p>The supplied relaxation-time passage motivates a separate forcing-switch-off experiment: preregister an energy or coherent-structure threshold and measure the time to reach and remain below it, with a specified hold interval, independent initial conditions, and spatial/time-step controls. No forcing quench or parameter quench was run in this suite. The medical association in the supplied passage is speculative and is not tested by these fluid calculations.</p>'
    body+='<h2>Completed-run inventory</h2>'+table(['Run','Status','End time','Peak / initial vorticity','Local valid prefix'],[[html.escape(r['run']),r['status'],fmt(r['last_t']),fmt(r['W_amplification']),fmt(r['last_local_prefix'])] for r in rows])
    body+='<h2>Files and sources</h2><p>For a fresh complete rerun, use <code>python reproduce.py --out NEW_DIRECTORY --backend gpu</code> (or <code>--backend cpu</code>). The wrapper copies only source and protocol files, then executes all 39 cases, checks and analysis. The destination must not exist. Install requirements.txt for CPU reproduction or requirements-gpu.txt for GPU reproduction. The fresh wrapper includes the six refinements for either backend. gpu-runtime.json records the actual CUDA package versions and device. CPU results use data/ and GPU results use data-gpu/; analysis prefers data-gpu/ when it exists. Execution is restartable by completed run. Do not call the low-level cached runners on a compact-results extraction: its result metadata exists but its NPZ files do not. Use the fresh-reproduction wrapper. Raw Fourier fields and diagnostic arrays accompany the full release archive; compact records contain hashes, settings and measurements. protocol.json records the design, summary.json records all comparisons, and verification.json records file checks.</p>'
    body+=f'<p><a href="{REPO}/tree/main/reports/navier-stokes-stress">Repository study</a> · <a href="{REPO}/releases/tag/{TAG}">Reproducible release</a></p>'
    body+='<p>Initial-field reference: <a href="https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/123303/1/non30.Ohkitani.au.pdf">Ohkitani (2018), Study of the 3D Euler equations using Clebsch potentials</a>. Only its smooth field definitions motivate this viscous experiment; its Euler conclusions are not imported. Benchmark context: <a href="https://how4.cenaero.be/content/bs1-dns-taylor-green-vortex-re1600">HiOCFD Taylor–Green benchmark</a>. No numerical reference-data match is claimed.</p>'
    css='body{font:17px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;background:#f3f5f7;color:#172b3a;margin:0}main{max-width:1080px;margin:auto;padding:45px 35px;background:white}h1{font-size:54px;line-height:1.1;margin:20px 0}h2{font-size:27px;margin-top:45px}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:2px;color:#475569}.lede{font-size:23px;max-width:850px}.callout{background:#edf4fa;border-left:5px solid #2563eb;padding:2px 25px 15px;margin:30px 0}figure{margin:35px 0}img{width:100%;height:auto}figcaption{font-size:14px;color:#526274}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}th,td{text-align:left;padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #dbe3ec;vertical-align:top}th{background:#edf4fa}.table{overflow:auto}a{color:#195cb8}code{background:#f0f3f7;padding:2px 5px}footer{font-size:13px;color:#526274}'
    (ROOT/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Navier–Stokes stress test</title><style>'+css+'</style><main>'+body+'<footer>Generated from saved measurements. '+time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M UTC',time.gmtime())+'</footer></main></html>',encoding='utf-8')
    lines=['# Navier–Stokes stress: smooth starts, strong gradients, explicit failure checks','',f'**{v["completed_runs"]}/{v["planned_runs"]} runs completed; {v["verified_fields"]} raw fields verified. No breakdown of Navier–Stokes is established.**','',f'[Readable report]({REPO}/releases/download/{TAG}/report.html) · [Full raw-data release]({REPO}/releases/tag/{TAG}) · [Protocol](protocol.json) · [All results](summary.json) · [Verification](verification.json)','','This is a new continuum-fluid experiment. It does not simulate molecules or isotope substitution. Both vortex starts are smooth and identical across grids; viscosity is positive and forcing is zero.','', '![Stress and spatial resolution](stress.png)','','## Finest-grid comparisons','','| Comparison | Last contiguous checked pass time | Final field difference | Final peak difference |','| --- | --- | --- | --- |']
    for c in grid:lines.append(f"| {c['coarse'].split('-e')[0]} → {c['fine'].split('-')[1]} | {fmt(c['last_contiguous_pass_time'])} | {100*c['points'][-1]['velocity_relative_L2']:.3f}% | {100*c['points'][-1]['W_relative_difference']:.3f}% |")
    lines+=['','Pass times use saved checkpoints and predeclared descriptive screens, not rigorous continuum error bounds. Late, unresolved results are retained explicitly as diagnostics.','',f'Maximum relative energy-budget residual: {maxbudget:.3g}. Maximum divergence RMS: {maxdiv:.3g}. Good conservation does not imply adequate spatial resolution.','','![Grid and time-step comparisons](convergence.png)','','## What ran','','- Kida–Pelz: viscosity 0.02, 0.005, 0.001, up to 256³; model time 2, including six explicitly recorded follow-up cases.','- Taylor–Green: viscosity 0.01 and 0.001 on 32³, 64³ and 96³; model time 4.','- Four perturbation directions at 0.1%; one direction repeated at 0.0001%, 0.1% and 1%, with exactly matched initial total energy; selected fine-grid and smaller-step follow-ups.','- Smaller-step controls, exact-solution checks, raw-field hashes and recomputed energy/enstrophy.','','Perturbations are broad velocity-field changes, not local molecular interventions. Their finite-time amplification does not establish an asymptotic Lyapunov exponent, a leader or a singularity.','','## Reproduce','','Install `requirements.txt` (CPU) or `requirements-gpu.txt` (GPU), then run `python reproduce.py --out NEW_DIRECTORY --backend cpu` (or `gpu`). The destination must not exist. This regenerates the 33 original cases and six refinements without confusing compact metadata with cached raw fields. For reanalysis of the full archive, run `analyze.py` and `report.py`. For reanalysis, extract the full release archive including `data-gpu/` and CPU controls in `data/`. Optional GPU execution: install `requirements-gpu.txt`, run `gpu_solver.py`, then `run_gpu.py` and `refine.py`. The extra six cases are recorded in `refinement-plan.json`. Raw NPZ arrays are release assets; compact JSON records remain in Git.','','## Sources and context','','- [Ohkitani (2018): smooth Kida–Pelz and Taylor–Green field definitions](https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/123303/1/non30.Ohkitani.au.pdf). Its inviscid conclusions are not applied to these viscous simulations.','- [Taylor–Green benchmark](https://how4.cenaero.be/content/bs1-dns-taylor-green-vortex-re1600). No reference-data validation is claimed.','- [Earlier spatial-resolution problems](../matched-stretch/README.md).','- [Separate molecular isotope study](../molecular/isotope-mass/README.md).','','No artificial extreme-mass molecular experiment was run after the user selected this fluid-equation direction.']
    lines+=['','## Supplied network images','','The supplied nine-node diagram describes saddle states of a whole system, and the phase-equation weight w_ij is coupling strength, not molecular mass. See the readable report for the source and the energy-decay constraint: this unforced zero-mean setup cannot sustain a nonzero recurrent cycle. Transient state-switching analysis and forced-flow experiments are proposed only, not completed tests.']
    (ROOT/'README.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
    print('Created report.html and README.md from measured results.')

if __name__=='__main__':main()
